An '''earworm''' or '''brainworm''' is a catchy piece of music that continuously occupies a person's mind even after it is no longer being played.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://example.org |title=Example}}</ref> Common descriptions include ''stuck song syndrome''.

Earworms are a common form of involuntary musical imagery. Most people report having one at least once a week, and they usually last a short time before fading on their own.

== Causes ==
Repetition, a simple melody and recent exposure all make a tune more likely to return. Songs with a fast tempo and an easy-to-remember melodic shape are reported more often than other songs.

Some listeners report that finishing the song, listening to it the whole way through, ends the loop. Others report that a puzzle or a task that needs attention, such as a word game, pushes the tune out.

The word is a direct translation of the German Ohrwurm, which has been used for the experience for many decades.

